|Important classification algorithms|

A. Logistic Regression
Despite its name, Logistic Regression is commonly used for classification, not ordinary numerical regression.
It estimates the probability that an observation belongs to a class.

When is it useful?
- Predicting customer churn.
- Predicting loan default.
- Predicting whether a support ticket belongs to a particular category.
Advantages: Fast, relatively simple, and interpretable.


B. Decision Tree Classifier
A Decision Tree makes predictions by asking a sequence of questions.

            Transaction amount
           Is it greater than ₹50,000?

|                                      |
No                                    Yes
 |                                     |
Check location                       Check frequency
Familiar or unusual?                 Unusually frequent?

# A Decision Tree learns which questions and splits help separate classes in the training data.

Advantages

- Easy to understand and visualize.
- Can learn nonlinear relationships.
- Usually needs less feature scaling than many other algorithms.

Limitations
- A deep tree can overfit: it may memorize training examples instead of learning patterns that generalize.
- Small data changes can sometimes produce a very different tree.

C. Random Forest Classifier  

A Random Forest combines predictions from multiple Decision Trees.
Imagine one tree flags a transaction as suspicious, while several other trees consider it legitimate. The forest combines their predictions to produce an overall result.

Advantages: Often performs well on structured/tabular data, handles nonlinear relationships, and is generally more robust than a single unrestricted tree.
Limitations: More computationally expensive than a small tree and less straightforward to explain as one simple set of rules.
